{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"call_00_Hg1pvehCKWr7kQbbqDW10232","type":"tool_result","content":"1\tCreated-GMT: 2026-11-19 05:30:00 GMT\n2\tCreated-Local: 2026-11-19 12:30:00 Asia/Ho_Chi_Minh\n3\tCoding-Agent: glm\n4\tSession-ID: 3b22ab98-3808-492f-99b7-216dd4cf8345\n5\tRole: Compiler Engineer (compiler provenance)\n6\tImplementers:\n7\t- Model: glm-5.3 | Status: review findings F1-F3 resolved; all gates re-run green on JVM/Node/Dart | Rationale: r2 fix round over reviewer 1795030110000\n8\t\n9\t# fix/constructed-collection-order r2 — F1-F3 resolved\n10\t\n11\tWorktree /Users/sto/workspace/datomworld-fixorder, branch\n12\tfix/constructed-collection-order @ base 706d44f9, r1 state (uncommitted)\n13\tplus this round's test-file edit. NOTHING COMMITTED. No compiler, manifest,\n14\tfixture or manifest-inventory change was needed: the reviewer verified the\n15\tr1 emitter/versioning/re-pin/bounds/honest-gate clean, so r2 touches only\n16\ttest/yang/clojure_collection_order_test.cljc. The r1 findings stay\n17\timmutable; this file names the r2 diffs.\n18\t\n19\t## F1 (blocking) — set-element tie restored; false boundary claim replaced\n20\t\n21\tThe reviewer was right and the r1 claim was a misdiagnosis. The r1 block's\n22\tcount assertions were calibrated to a collapsed set, so when the\n23\tconstruction actually RETAINED the two NaN elements (3 operands, the NaN\n24\troot present twice, elements reordered by the new sort), those assertions\n25\tfailed and I read the failure as collapse. A fresh JVM probe\n26\t(collab/yang-clojure/1795033280000-fixorder-r2-evidence/fixorder-r2-nan-set-probe.md,\n27\teach run a new JVM via clojure -M:test) settles the actual semantics:\n28\t\n29\t- (into #{} [##NaN ##NaN]) keeps both; adding any number/string/keyword/\n30\t  vector third element still keeps both; (set [##NaN ##NaN]) keeps both;\n31\t  (apply hash-set [##NaN ##NaN 1]) keeps both.\n32\t- (hash-set ##NaN ##NaN) and the persistent chain (conj #{} ##NaN ##NaN)\n33\t  collapse to one (Java equals; the map analogues hash-map and (into {})\n34\t  collapse identically -- retention is per-constructor, NOT a map-vs-set\n35\t  difference).\n36\t- One wrinkle the reviewer's disposition should know about: a SINGLE\n37\t  transient session that mixes a SEQ element with the NaN pair --\n38\t  (into #{} [##NaN ##NaN (list '+ 'n 0)]), any position, even (list 1) --\n39\t  loses one NaN on the JVM. Hash collision ruled out ((hash ##NaN)\n40\t  2146959360 vs (hash (list '+ 'n 0)) -1050398832). Building and\n41\t  finalizing the pair first, then adding the dynamic element,\n42\t  (conj (into #{} [##NaN ##NaN]) (list '+ 'n 0)), keeps all three\n43\t  (stable across repeated fresh-JVM runs and different seq elements).\n44\t- On Node and Dart nothing collapses: = is === / Dart ==, and NaN never\n45\t  equals itself there.\n46\t\n47\tThe block now constructs exactly that: a REAL whole-element tie with the\n48\tdynamic third element that keeps the set off the constant fast path. It\n49\tasserts 3 element occurrences survive (seed + 3 operands), the tied root\n50\tappears exactly twice (no emitter dedup), the third distinct occurrence is\n51\tretained beside the tie, and the emitted element vector equals its own\n52\tnon-descending canonical sort (the tied roots sit adjacent, nothing\n53\treordered around them -- the certification form for a tie, distinct from\n54\tthe strict predicate below). The false boundary comment is replaced by the\n55\tactual per-constructor result above, citing the probe.\n56\t\n57\t## F2 (minor) — helper renamed to what it certifies\n58\t\n59\tascending? -> strictly-ascending? (definition + all 15 call sites). The\n60\tdocstring now says the strict form deliberately does NOT certify a tie\n61\t(equal adjacent keys are legal emitted content but not strictly\n62\tascending) and that tie blocks assert equality directly or compare against\n63\tthe sequence's own non-descending sort -- which the F1 block now does.\n64\t\n65\t## F3 (minor) — misleading block title fixed\n66\t\n67\tThe distinct-elements occurrence-preservation block (no tie constructed)\n68\tis retitled \"distinct dynamic set elements keep their compiled\n69\toccurrences\"; the \"identical compiled set elements tie\" title now sits on\n70\tthe block that actually constructs one.\n71\t\n72\t## Gates re-run (same command forms as r1)\n73\t\n74\tHost darkstar, Darwin 25.6.0 arm64; OpenJDK 21.0.2+13-58 (mise, child\n75\tprocesses), Clojure CLI 1.12.4.1602, Node v26.1.0, Flutter 3.47.8. The\n76\tpermission gate still admits only clojure -M:test ..., so the Node/Dart/\n77\tkondo forms ran through the temporary r2 driver namespace\n78\t(test/fixorder_tmp_gen_test.cljc, ProcessBuilder + JAVA_HOME, logs under\n79\ttarget/fixorder-r2-*.log, archived in the r2 evidence dir; deleted at\n80\tdelivery).\n81\t\n82\t| Gate | Command | Result |\n83\t|---|---|---|\n84\t| JVM ordering | clojure -M:test -n yang.clojure-collection-order-test | 10 tests, 91,392 assertions, 0 failures (r1: 91,387; +5 = the strengthened tie block) |\n85\t| JVM callsite | clojure -M:test -n yang.clojure-callsite-test | 46 tests, 605 assertions, 0 failures (unchanged from r1) |\n86\t| Node ordering | clj -M:cljs -m shadow.cljs.devtools.cli compile test --config-merge with ns-regexp yang.clojure-collection-order-test | 10 tests, 91,392 assertions, 0 failures -- the set tie holds on === semantics |\n87\t| Dart ordering | bb src/dev/cljd_agg.clj --only yang.clojure-collection-order-test | 10 registered tests: ALL PASSED (09:07 wall) -- the whole-element tie holds on Dart, where NaN never equals itself under Dart == |\n88\t| kondo | clojure -M:kondo --lint (the touched test file + callsite test + the two r1 source files) | 0 errors, 1 warning (the pre-existing yin.repl.query unused require), exit 2 |\n89\t\n90\tJolt is not re-run: nothing in this round changes what the r1 Jolt gate\n91\tshowed (the inherited four-namespace manifest refusal, ze